.TH newscache 8 "June 2004"

.SH NAME
newscache \- Cache for Network News Transport Protocol (NNTP)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B newscache
.RI "[\-h] [\-v] [\-f] [\-c config-file] [\-i] [\-t] [\-d] [\-n] [\-p] [\-o]"
.br
.SH DESCRIPTION
NewsCache is a free cache server for USENET News. NewsCache acts to news reading clients like a news server, except that it stores only those articles that have been requested by at least one client. NewsCache targets problems of the current News System like network bandwidth consumption or the IO load caused by news clients. NewsCache acts to the upstream news server like a news reader programm.

.TP
It supports principles the following modes (for a detailed description read ServerSpec Configuration Options below):
.TP
.I "cached"
NewsCache store every retrieved article in his internal cache. If this article is retrieved by another news reader client, then NewsCache can get the article from his cache instead of retrieving this article again from the upstream newsserver.
.TP
.I "not-cached"
NewsCache act as a "proxy" and does'nt store retrieved article in his local cache. This is usefull for combined multiple local newsserver configuration.
.TP
.I "offline"
NewsCache does'nt connect to the upstream newsserver. Only the specified newsgroups are retrieved by
.BR updatenews(8)
and stored in the cache. This is usefull in dialup configurations. The news reader clients can only access this newsgroups.
.TP
.I "semi-offline, cached"
The overview informations are loaded with
.BR updatenews(8)
and the articles retrieved by NewsCache, if a news reader client request it. The article is stored in the internal cache.

.SH OPTIONS
This program follows the usual GNU command line syntax, with long
options starting with two dashes (`-').
A summary of options is included below.
For a complete description, see the Info files.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Show summary of options.
.TP
.B \-v, \-\-version
Show version of program.
.TP
.B \-f \-\-fqdn
Print what newscache thinks is the fully qualified domain name.
.TP
.B \-c \-\-configuration config-file
Read config-file instead of
.IR @SYSCONFDIR@/newscache.conf .
.TP
.B \-i \-\-inetd
newscache is used with inetd and read from stdin.
.TP
.B \-t \-\-tls
As \-i, for a client that uses TLS from the first byte (port 563,
nntps): standard input is the client's socket, and newscache does the
TLS handshake with TLSCertFile and TLSKeyFile (see
.BR newscache.conf (5)).
The certificate and key are loaded before newscache switches to
Username, so it may be started as root. The client is matched in the
AccessList by its address (not as stdin, as with \-i). In standalone
mode, TLSListenTo does this for a second listening socket.
.TP
.B \-d \-\-debug
Do not detach: stay in the foreground (the same as \-n).
.TP
.B \-n \-\-no\-fork
Do not fork into the background, as for systemd (Type=simple) or a
container; the same as \-d.
.TP
.B \-p \-\-print-parameter
Print current parameter settings; with TLSCertFile set, it loads the
certificate, key and TLSPriority first and exits with status 2 if that
fails. Upstream server passwords are not shown;
each one is replaced by the comment
.IR "# Password set, not shown" .
.TP
.B \-o \-\-configuration-options
Print all options specified in the configure phase. It doesn't read
the configuration file, so it works when that is missing or has an error.

.SH SIGNALS

.TP 
.B SIGTERM, SIGINT

These signals instruct NewsCache to abort any existing transfers, close
all connections and terminate the process. A session process answers
its client "400 ... service discontinued" and ends, also while it waits
for the client. If the target of the signal is the daemon, it sends
SIGTERM to all its session processes and ends. SIGPIPE is ignored.

.TP 
.B SIGHUP

This  signal instruct NewsCache to reread its configuration file
.I @SYSCONFDIR@/newscache.conf
\. With TLSListenTo it loads TLSCertFile and TLSKeyFile again, as
Username; listening addresses change only with a restart.

.SH NOTES
Running NewsCache in
.B inetd
mode requires a correct inetd configuration. An example configuration line looks like:
.PP
.B news stream tcp nowait news /usr/sbin/newscache -i
.PP
and for TLS clients (the key readable by user news, or root in the
user field: newscache switches to Username after loading it):
.PP
.B nntps stream tcp nowait root /usr/sbin/newscache -t
.PP

.SH NEWSGROUP TIMES
The timestamp returned by
.B LIST ACTIVE.TIMES
and used by
.B NEWGROUPS
is when NewsCache first saw the group in an upstream active list and stored
it locally. It is not the upstream group's creation time: NewsCache does not
request upstream creation times and does not backdate the timestamp. The
creator field is the generic value
.B news
because the actual creator is not available from the active list.

.SH DIAGNOSTICS
NewsCache logs diagnostics messages with the syslog priority debug. If NewsCache is configured with the
.B \-\-enable\-debug
option, he produce much more messages (for details see the INSTALL file). 
.B newscache \-p
prints the configuration as NewsCache has read it, and
.B newscache \-o
the options NewsCache was built with.  In some error situation
you can try to create a core dump file or debug a client process with
gdb (for details see the documentation directory).

.SH FILES
@SYSCONFDIR@/newscache.conf
.br
@SBINDIR@/newscache
.br
@SBINDIR@/newscacheclean
.br
@SBINDIR@/updatenews

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR newscache.conf (5),
.BR newscacheclean (8),
.BR updatenews (8).

